WEBVTT

00:06:29.395 --> 00:06:32.728
today has samples
from over 15 million people.

00:06:32.752 --> 00:06:36.263
23andMe, the second-largest
genealogical database,

00:06:36.287 --> 00:06:38.724
has samples from over 10 million people.

00:06:38.748 --> 00:06:42.343
So now, maybe you don't need
these James Bond-worthy techniques

00:06:42.367 --> 00:06:43.961
of collecting DNA

00:06:43.985 --> 00:06:46.937
if we're willingly handing it over
to private companies

00:06:46.961 --> 00:06:49.410
and even paying for the honor of doing it.

00:06:49.807 --> 00:06:53.138
Well, what could you do
with a sample of someone's DNA?

00:06:53.162 --> 00:06:54.679
In the United States and China,

00:06:54.703 --> 00:06:57.178
researchers are working
on using DNA samples

00:06:57.202 --> 00:06:59.520
to build images of people's faces.

00:06:59.544 --> 00:07:03.513
So if you pair DNA
with facial recognition technology,